-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  std_customio.adam - System.CustomIO's streams (PaskalCPP's std_customio)

  An IO is a stream behind a table of callbacks (IOInterface). The IO
  routines call through the table and keep the state: a memory IO over the
  caller's memory (read / write within its size, read-only), a memory IO
  over its own growing buffer, an IO a program defines itself with
  CreateIO, the nil handle, and a file IO in each FileMode (its file in
  repo\bin\output, its name not ASCII; a missing file and a folder give
  nil). IOTruncate shortens and lengthens owned memory, caller memory
  (within its block) and a file IO (the position kept, zeros where it
  grows); read-only IOs, an IO without the callback, a negative size and
  nil refuse. A file IO open is never renamed under it (Windows refuses
  the rename, as for C's fopen): its resize still reaches it. pcpp's Linux
  rows (a rename while open, in the system temp folder) wait for linux64.

  Each row prints ok or FAIL with its name; the last is `done <failures>`.
*******************************************************************************/

